    "body": "> [!WARNING]\n> **Proof of concept, like #2508, and not close to done.** It exists to show\n> what the standard library looks like once modules are real values, and to\n> find out what that costs. Read \"Known gaps\" before reviewing anything else.\n\nNinth of the stack, on top of #2508. The standard library becomes modules,\nand ships the vocabulary the implicits feature needs.\n\n```\nList.map([1, 2, 3], fun x -> x * 2)          -- [2, 4, 6]\nString.of_int(42) ++ String.uppercase(\"ok\")  -- \"42OK\"\nFloat.sqrt(9.0) >. Float.pi                  -- false\nOption.map((Some(2), fun x -> x + 1))        -- Some(3)\n\nlet implicit A = ShowInt in\nlet show = fun (implicit S : SHOW, x : S.T) -> S.show(x) in\nshow(3)                                      -- \"3\"\n```\n\n## What it does\n\n- **Six modules**: `List` (all 65 list functions), `String`, `Int`, `Float`,\n  `Option`, `Pair`. The list family is the one that crowds the namespace\n  worst: `map`, `filter`, `length`, `find` and `head` are all top-level\n  names today.\n- **Every flat name stays.** `String.length` and `string_length` are the\n  same function, so no program, slide or exercise changes meaning. The\n  modules are the front door from here on; nothing is deprecated in this PR.\n- **Both halves are derived from the same records.** A module builtin is an\n  ordinary constant whose type is a signature and whose value is a module,\n  and the signature's member types and the module's member values come from\n  the same builtin, so a member cannot drift from the function it names. A\n  member is the implementation, not a copy: it is the term the flat name is\n  bound to.\n- **`SHOW` and `ORD` ship as signature types**, with an instance per base\n  type: `ShowInt`, `ShowFloat`, `ShowBool`, `ShowString`, `OrdInt`,\n  `OrdFloat`, `OrdString`. Each carries a manifest type member, since\n  resolution selects on it.\n\n## Two decisions worth arguing with\n\n**The instances are not ambient.** A program marks what it wants, one line\nper instance. Making them ambient is a language decision rather than a\nlibrary one, and it needs a priority or coherence rule first: with none, a\nprogram that defines its own instance for a base type under any other name\nmakes every call ambiguous against the shipped one. Twelve of the implicits\ntests change meaning when they are ambient, which is the same warning from\nthe other side. The cost is a `let implicit A = ShowInt in` line, which is\nwhat the Module Implicits slide already writes.\n\n**No `EQ`.** Polymorphic `==` already compares two values of a type, so an\nequality signature earns its keep only once a type can choose its own\nequality, which wants the sharing constraint #2495 lacks.\n\n## Two prerequisites this found, fixed where they belong\n\nBoth are in #2493, with tests, because plain source hits them:\n\n- A module member's name is its label, but substitution renamed it away from\n  capture like any binder, so `M.x` stopped finding it. The stepper\n  substitutes the whole builtin environment, so any module with a member\n  named after a builtin lost that member.\n- A capitalized module name parses as a constructor, and a known constructor\n  won, so a module whose name matched one could not be projected at all. The\n  JSON type contributes `List`, `Int`, `String`, `Float` and `Bool` to every\n  program's constructor namespace, which made exactly the five most useful\n  stdlib names unusable: `module List = { let f = 1 } in List.f` was an\n  error. Projecting a constructor means nothing, so the module now wins in\n  dot position, and `List([Int(2)])` still builds JSON.\n\nThe one change here is in the evaluator: a member holding a fixpoint has to\nunroll, so a projection judges a definition the way a variable lookup does,\nsharing one predicate. Only a module that came out of the environment can\nhit it, which is why it is in this PR rather than #2493.\n\n## Known gaps\n\n1. **Cost per mention.** A module out of the environment is copied on each\n   mention, so `List.map` copies a 65-member module where `map` copies one\n   fixpoint. I have not measured it against a hot loop, and if it matters the\n   answer is probably to bind the library once per program rather than to\n   hand it out of the environment, which needs a prelude mechanism that does\n   not exist.\n2. **The grouping is partial.** The tuple-label operations, the ADT helpers,\n   the `Ord` inversion, `print`, the SInt and Nat families and the remaining\n   conversions are still only flat. `Bool` has nothing worth grouping yet.\n3. **`Int`, `Float` and `String` cannot be *declared* as modules in the text\n   grammar**, since those are base-type tokens: `module Int = {\u2026}` does not\n   parse, though the shipped ones are fine because nothing declares them in\n   source. The editor accepts it.\n4. **Nothing is deprecated.** Two names for every function is the price of\n   not breaking programs, and there is no mechanism to mark the flat ones\n   legacy.\n5. **No `List` for other collections.** There is no `Set`, `Map` or `Seq`\n   because there are no such builtins; this PR groups what exists.\n6. **`Option`'s type member is `Option`'s sum type**, so `Option.T` names it,\n   but the module cannot expose the constructors: constructors declared in a\n   module are not members of it (#2243).\n\n## Verification\n\n`make dev` clean \u00b7 `make ci-check` clean under the release profile \u00b7\n`make test-quick` 3845 green \u00b7 new suites `Statics.BuiltinsModules` (11) and\n`Evaluator.BuiltinsModules` (10), including that a member equals its flat\nfunction and that a recursive member unrolls through a projection \u00b7 CLI\nprobes for every module and for both signatures.\n\nExpect the same two per-seed `Full tests` failures as the rest of the stack,\nboth unrelated and both dev's: the cyclic-alias crash in normalization and\nthe `:+` printer divergence explained on #2493.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
